  "cil_liability": true,
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed dwelling, by reason of the poor indicative design presented and in the absence of any robust technical assessments provided, would fail to provide adequate daylight and sunlight provision, poor opportunities for natural ventilation of the internal living space and poor quality outlook and an undue sense of enclosure. In addtion, the proposed development would fail to provide adequate internal floor-to-ceiling heights, resulting in poor daylight penetration, ventilation and a sense of space and likely lead to overheating of the internal living space. Accordingy, any future design of the proposed dwelling would unlikley provide satisfactory living conditions for future residents. In addition, the private outdoor amenity provision would be of a poor quality, would not be genuinely fit for purpose and it has not been demonstrated that the amenity space proposed would benefit from good levels of direct sunlight. The proposed development would accordingly fail to comply with Policy D6 of the London Plan, Policy PG13 of the Residential Design SPG and Chapter 12 of the NPPF.",
    "The proposed change of use from Class B8 to a residential dwelling (Class C3), including the constrained and narrow access to the existing building, would result in a substantial increase in the comings and goings through this access point. Given the location of the ground floor windows of no. 55A Park Road, the proposal would cause harm to the residential amenity of the occupants of this property, due to increased noise and impacts on privacy, to the detriment of the living conditions of the occupants of no. 55A. The proposal would therefore fail to comply with the requirements of the Residential Design SPG and Chapter 12 of the NPPF.",
    "In the absence of an executed Unilateral Undertaking to ensure that the development is car capped i.e. current and/or future occupants of the development would not be able to obtain car parking permits, the proposed development would be highly likely to lead to inconsiderate parking on surrounding streets, which would, in turn, compromise highway safety. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy T4 of the London Plan 2021 and policy DM10 of the LDF Core Strategy 2012."
  ],
